          %meta{:content => "application/xhtml+xml;charset=utf-8", "http-equiv" => "Content-type"}/
          %title
          %p This entry is about the monograph:
          %ul
            %li#BorceuxJanelidze2001
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Francis+Borceux"} Francis Borceux
                and
                = succeed ":" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/George+Janelidze"} George Janelidze
              %p
                %br/
              %p
                %strong Galois Theories
              %p
                %br/
              %p
                Camb. Stud. Adv. Math.
                %strong 72
              %p Cambridge University Press (2001)
              %p
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9780511619939"} doi:10.1017/CBO9780511619939
          %p
            on
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Galois+theory"} Galois theory
          %blockquote
            %p
              The following starts out with an edited and adapted version of a review of the book by
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tim+Porter"} Tim Porter
              that appeared in Proceedings of the Edinburgh Mathematical Society (Series 2) (2002), 45 : 761-762.
          %p The developments that lead to this book are due to some deeply based analogies between some initially very different looking mathematical theories, one in algebra, another in topology.
          %p
            Galois originally developed some of elements of what was to become Galois theory in an attempt to understand polynomial equations, continuing work of Abel and others. In modern language, working over a base field,
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                  %mo ⊂
                  %mi L
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K\subset L
            is a Galois extension when every element of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            is the root of a polynomial in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K[X]
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L[X]
            into linear factors and all of whose roots are simple. The group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Gal
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi L
                  %mo :
                  %mi K
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Gal[L:K]
            of this extension is the group of field automorphisms of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            which fix all elements of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            and the classical Galois theorem asserts that when
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            = succeed "-vector" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi Gal
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi L
                  %mo :
                  %mi K
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G\subseteq Gal[L:K]
            of the Galois group classify the intermediate field extensions
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                    %mo ⊆
                    %mi M
                    %mo ⊆
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K\subseteq M\subseteq L
          %p
            The second theory is that of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/covering+space"} covering spaces
            in topology. A covering map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi α
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha : X\to B
            is one that has the property that every point of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            has an open neighbourhood whose inverse image by
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi α
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha
            is a disjoint union of open subsets each of which is mapped homeomorphically onto it by
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi α
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                    %mo :
                    %mover
                      %mi B
                      %mo ˜
                    %mo →
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p : \widetilde{B}\to B
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            are quotients of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi B
                      %mo ˜
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \widetilde{B}
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            (under suitable local conditions).
          %p
            This topological theory of covering spaces has some similarities to Galois theory. Again one has automorphism groups and a correspondence between intermediate structures (this time quotients not subobjects) and perhaps some notion of splitting - there is an open cover of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            over each part of which the covering splits up as a family of isomorphic pieces.
          %p These two theories, thus, do look vaguely similar, but automorphism groups are very common in mathematics and even that sort of ‘Galois’ correspondence is not that uncommon, so surely any similarities must not be due to anything really deep! The story of how the deep connections between them became apparent is quite long and here is not the place to explore it in any detail. It involves function spaces and Riemann surfaces as well as a lot else that is central to modern pure maths (if you want a good source for the theory see the beautiful book (see below) by Douady and Douady).
          %p
            Borceux and Janelidze’s book,
            = succeed "," do
              %em Galois theories
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/SGA1"} SGA1
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pursuing+Stacks"} Pursuing Stacks
          %p This book explores the connections between these theories, searching for further cases of the general ‘scenario’ and tries to strip back the superficial structure to reveal aspects of what are the essential features of all these theories.
          %p
            The book assumes a certain knowledge of algebra and general topology, together with some familiarity with the
            %strong elementary
            language of category theory (categories, functors, natural transformations, limits and adjoint functors). Its starts with a short trip through the theory of field extensions, then goes on to look at Grothendieck’s extension of this to algebras.
          %p
            Chapter 3 handles infinitary Galois theory. Here
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/profinite+space"} profinite spaces
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/profinite+group"} profinite groups
            are introduced. They are also useful in the following chapter where the extension of Galois theory to commutative rings (due to Magid, see reference below) is treated. The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pierce+spectrum"} Pierce spectrum
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stone+duality"} Stone duality
            are handled clearly and simply laying the base for Magid’s profinite Galois groupoid.
          %p With that first layer of categorical abstraction (Janelidze’s abstract categorical Galois theory) in place, other applications can be explored. Given the introduction to the area in this review, it may seem strange that covering maps are only introduced in Chapter 6, but here they can be very neatly described and handled, beautifully illustrating and enriching the earlier abstraction.
          %p The final chapter describes one of the most important recent advances in topos theory, giving an introduction to the Joyal–Tierney classification of Grothendieck toposes as sheaves on localic groupoids. The book ends with a look at other directions the theory has taken beyond those handled in detail. This section is particularly valuable as it should set the scene for future research.
          %p It introduces many deep important concepts of algebra and category theory, introduces, motivates and uses in an interesting way. As one would expect, this means that the later chapters are sometimes much harder going than the earlier ones, but the writing and structure of the book is such that the transition is fairly gradual.
          %p Some researchers will probably feel that some of the exercises should have been worked out in detail, but given the starting assumptions of the authors, and the reasonably good set of references, there were bound to be such omissions.
